title: FirstLoop_Babushka
---
In front of you sits a woman whose old age shines through her shriveled skin and trembling hands.
One of those shaky hands rests firmly on an old leather bag, while the other cradles a dull looking Matryoshka urn. 

<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
    Whatever is in the bag and whoever is in the doll mean a lot to her. 
    She holds the items close to her, as firmly as she is able to, as if the wind could blow them away at any moment. She seems terrified to lose them. 
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
    Whoever is in the urn was not native to Solster. Only those born here have their urns painted in their semblance, and this one looks like a typical Matryoshka doll.
    The shiny painted lines on the urn tell you it was painted quite recently. Their shakiness tells you the old woman painted it herself.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
    The straps that are attached to the leather bag have almost completely rotted off; it won't stay in one piece for long. 
    It could be easily replaced or mended right now, but people in Solster only fix something after it is broken, not while it's still ‘functional’. 
<< endif >>

Her gaze is fixed on a mine entrance close by, her mind seems occupied. 
It takes a while for the woman to notice you, shrouded in her own darkness, but when she does you are hit with a smile as bright as the sun outside Solster. 
Like a moth to a flame, you are enraptured by it. You’re in the presence of a soul warm like a star.
Baba: Oh. Bless mothers threads. Hello there, child.
Baba: It is not often mother blesses us with visitors from outside the void.
Her bright smile mellows down a little while speaking but her eyes remain ever so radiant.
Baba: And on the day of the parade, nonetheless! 
Baba: Oh! How incredibly wonderful!
The woman's glowing smile returns fully yet again, filling your soul with warmth.
Baba: It has been years since we have had someone from outside of Solster join the parade.
She looks down at the urn cradled in her lap.
Baba: How incredibly wonderful, and today of all days..

-> “Well, I was actually born here in Solster.”
    Sasha: “Well, I was actually born here in Solster.”
	Sasha: “I’m back fo-....”
-> Say nothing
    Sasha: (Say nothing.)
    The woman looks up at you, excited to keep talking

Baba: Most people around here won't be as kind to an outsider like you 
Baba: An odd thing really, I have never understood. Our most cherished things come from the outside.
Baba: Without brave Dynnra we would have never learned the power of the void.
Baba: Without the radiant Elegy and the Everwatcher we would be lost in the dark, we would have no power, and no one to look up to for protection.

-> “Yes, I understand, but like I said I’m from Solster”
    Sasha: “Yes, I understand, but like I said I’m from Solster.”
    The woman pauses for a second, but only to continue speaking as if she did not hear you. She definitely did hear you though.
-> Say nothing and accept the situation.

Babushka: Who knowns
Babushka: Maybe you will be the next outsider to make a difference

Baba: Who knows, maybe you will be the next outsider to make a difference. 
Trying to convince the woman of your connection to Solster is hopeless. She is so sure of her belief that you are not from Solster. 
Maybe she is not that far off. It has been a long time since you have been here. Did the outside world really change you beyond recognition?
Baba: Since you are new here, many things must still be confusing to you.
Baba: If you have any questions about Solster and its customs, I will be here for you, child.
Baba: Don't be afraid to ask something, anything.
Baba: It can get a bit lonely sometimes… haha….. 
Her laugh hides pain. The more you look at her, the more you slowly start to remember her. 
It was hard to recognise her at first with her discolored, marbling and plastic looking skin. Her nose seems sharper and her eyes and cheeks are sunken in. 
But behind all of that sits a woman who would always give out bandaids and quick prayers to the kids who scraped their knees while playing. 
The woman who would watch over them while their parents worked hard to put food on the table. 
The woman whose freshly baked pastries would sit in the window sill, spreading a sweet smell of cinnamon, vanilla and roasted nuts throughout the streets of the River District.
Baba: Oh, but I do make sure to catch the parade today, so let’s not get too carried away with chatting.
Baba: It is quite a sight to behold I tell you. 
The woman looks back at the mine, before quickly turning her head around to face you again. 
You look around as well. The streets of the river district seem more quiet than usual, it looks almost abandoned. 
You prefer to think it is because of the parade, but deep down you know it's not. The Silence feels heavier than it has to be. Something is wrong here.
Baba: Oh mother, forgive me, I almost forgot! The name is Maacah, …. but please just call me Babushka.
Reaching her old age is rare in Solster, especially in the river district. Therefore she’s become the district's shared “grandmother”. Much like she was its babysitter before.


===
